                "entry_id": "1c5e96db-79c3-3fc6-a58c-ce0c5b3ccfa9",
                "word": "littleness",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "7eebdfae-507b-4da4-9621-b0f8a1d5f417",
                        "entry_id": "1c5e96db-79c3-3fc6-a58c-ce0c5b3ccfa9",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "littlenesses"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e07c07ba-66e8-35c5-bbfa-e489ce244bfd",
                        "entry_id": "1c5e96db-79c3-3fc6-a58c-ce0c5b3ccfa9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The property of being little, smallness."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b07fff2b-bd43-4f58-96c7-0f8a70746802",
                                "sense_id": "e07c07ba-66e8-35c5-bbfa-e489ce244bfd",
                                "text": "For although the Queen had ordered a little Equipage of all things neceſſary while I was in her Service, yet my Ideas were wholly taken up with what I ſaw on every ſide of me, and winked at my own Littleneſs as People do at their own Faults."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4cd08c7b-300a-43a7-9288-804e4e45fa83",
                                "sense_id": "e07c07ba-66e8-35c5-bbfa-e489ce244bfd",
                                "text": "His littleness didn't bother him, except when he needed to get something off the top shelf."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "340137d1-7f5d-4677-b520-0b03b36c4878",
                                "sense_id": "e07c07ba-66e8-35c5-bbfa-e489ce244bfd",
                                "text": "1757, Edmund Burke, A Philosophical Enquiry into the Origin of Our Ideas of the Sublime and Beautiful, Part III, Sect. 21, pp. 161-162,\nLittleness, merely as such, has nothing contrary to the idea of beauty. The humming bird both in shape and colouring yields to none of the winged species, of which it is the least; and perhaps his beauty is enhanced by his smallness."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "719ac8ac-0eda-3675-ac6d-1d35ef283633",
                        "entry_id": "1c5e96db-79c3-3fc6-a58c-ce0c5b3ccfa9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Smallness of spirit; pettiness."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "0c0d3471-ddaa-4148-99f7-83be7e01b1f7",
                                "sense_id": "719ac8ac-0eda-3675-ac6d-1d35ef283633",
                                "text": "Court, city, church are all shops of smallwares;\nAll having blown to sparks their noble fire,\nAnd drawn their sound gold ingot into wire;\nAll trying by a love of littleness\nTo make abridgments, and to draw to less\nEven that nothing which at first we were;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8e920b62-7937-4b45-9556-4b1873f9dc96",
                                "sense_id": "719ac8ac-0eda-3675-ac6d-1d35ef283633",
                                "text": "So unlike what a man should be!—None of that upright integrity, that strict adherence to truth and principle, that disdain of trick and littleness, which a man should display in every transaction of his life."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "32e408db-f6af-454f-8fcc-7adc46ea460d",
                                "sense_id": "719ac8ac-0eda-3675-ac6d-1d35ef283633",
                                "text": "Tyler […] has been called a mediocre man; but this is unwarranted flattery. He was a politician of monumental littleness."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a503ba29-4c41-49ba-9857-bf8ecc2e7bc3",
                                "sense_id": "719ac8ac-0eda-3675-ac6d-1d35ef283633",
                                "text": "There is no doubt about what is not great, no race of men have such obvious littlenesses. They live in a narrow world so far as their human intercourse goes; their researches involve infinite attention and an almost monastic seclusion; and what is left over is not very much."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
